
Read this before touching anything. Full spec: `docs/SPEC.md`.

## What this is

A **research terminal** for Kalshi CS2 prediction markets. It assembles a
statistical dossier per match. It is **not** an edge-finder, not a fair-value
model, and never places orders.

## Stack

Python 3.11+ · Flask + Jinja2 · PostgreSQL (Neon in dev) · ECharts · APScheduler
· curl_cffi + selectolax for scraping · pytest.

No ORM. Raw SQL by design — this project needs hand-written analytical queries.
No JS build step. Server-rendered HTML only.

## Rules that are not negotiable

1. **Append-only.** Any table named `*_snapshots` is INSERT-only. Never UPDATE
   one. Current state comes from the `v_*_latest` views. This is what makes
   historical reconstruction possible; breaking it silently destroys the
   project's second phase.

2. **Never split a Kalshi event ticker to get team abbreviations.** They are
   concatenated with no delimiter and are variable length: `BSTAUND` = `BSTA`
   + `UND`, but `EXMANAMAI` = `EXMANA` + `MAI`. Read the event's child markets
   — each ends in one team's abbreviation and its title carries the full name.

3. **Derive match start as `expiration_time - 48h`** — NOT `close_time`, and
   not by parsing the ticker's HHMM (US Eastern, easy to get wrong).

   These markets set `can_close_early`, so Kalshi **rewrites `close_time`**
   to the moment a winner was declared once the market settles. Measured on
   a real settled market, `close_time - 48h` put the start **47.3 hours
   early**; `expiration_time - 48h` hit it exactly. The old rule worked for
   two months because it is correct while a market is open, and failed
   invisibly the moment anything settled — 2,279 of 2,296 historical events
   failed to resolve, and several bound to unrelated matches.

   `close_time` stays as the fallback when `expiration_time` is absent.

4. **The display contract.** Every rate rendered in the UI carries its sample
   size, effective sample size, and staleness. Every shrunk figure shows the
   raw record beside it (`0-6 (adj. 31%)`). Every roster-derived stat shows
   coverage (`3/5 resolved`). No exceptions — this is what stops the tool
   lying by omission.

5. **`stats/` modules are pure functions.** They take rows and return values.
   No database access, no network, no globals. That is what makes them
   testable.

6. **NOT YET IMPLEMENTED — do not go looking for it.** The spec calls for a
   `Fetcher` protocol in `edgedesk/fetch/base.py` that sources receive by
   injection. That module does not exist, and both `kalshi.py` and `bo3.py`
   instantiate `httpx.Client` directly. Introduce it when a third source
   arrives and the caching/retry duplication justifies it — until then,
   follow the existing pattern rather than half-building the abstraction.

7. **Reserved words.** Postgres reserves `window` and `values` — the columns
   are `stat_window` and `source_values`. Don't "fix" them back.

## bo3.gg API traps (verified 2026-09)

- **Only `[eq]` and `[in]` filters work.** Range operators like `[gte]` are
  **silently ignored** — a date filter returns the entire 79k-row table
  instead of erroring. Never rely on a date filter; use `status` + `sort` +
  a client-side cutoff with early termination.
- Match objects carry `team1_id` / `team2_id` **as integers only**. Team
  names require a separate `/teams` lookup.
- Game scores are `winner_clan_score` / `loser_clan_score` with in-game
  **clan names**, which do not reliably equal registered team names
  ("Diamant" vs "Diamant Esports"). Fuzzy-map them and keep the raw strings.
- **No CT/T splits and no half-time scores exist anywhere in bo3.** Round
  differential is computable; side splits are not. Do not spec them.
- **`/players` works (~20k rows); `/players_stats`, `/game_players`,
  `/rosters` and `/team_players` are all 404**, and game objects carry NO
  player keys. bo3 gives identity and current team membership; it has no
  performance data whatsoever. Ratings require HLTV. Probed 2026-09 — do
  not re-probe.
- **`filter[players.team_id][eq]` is silently ignored** (returned five
  unrelated teams). Pull the whole player table and index client-side.
- `limit` is ignored for small values — read the page size from the
  envelope's `total.limit`.
- **`filter[games.match_id][in]` IS honoured** (probed 2026-09: five ids
  requested, exactly those five returned). Batch games 50 matches per call.
  But you MUST paginate — 50 matches averages ~130 games against a page size
  of 100, and the overflow is dropped silently, which reads as a match that
  only played two maps rather than as an error.

## bo3 endpoint inventory (probed 2026-09 — do not re-probe)

Exist: `/matches` (79.7k) · `/teams` (8.4k) · `/games` (143.9k) ·
`/tournaments` (3.1k) · `/players` (20.3k) · `/series` (741) ·
`/stages` (10.3k) · `/maps` (46) · `/countries` (254) ·
`/disciplines` (8) · `/streams` (2.5k).

404, confirmed absent: `/vetoes` `/picks` `/bans` (veto data is a PERMANENT
gap, not an unexplored one) · `/players_stats` `/player_stats`
`/game_players` `/match_players` `/team_players` (no performance data
anywhere — ratings require HLTV) · `/rankings` `/ratings` `/standings` ·
`/rosters` `/lineups` `/transfers` · `/odds` `/predictions`.

Field names differ where you would not expect: tournaments use `name`,
stages use `title`. Matches carry `tournament_id`, `stage_id` and
`round_id`.

Use `scripts/explore_bo3.py` to check anything else; it verifies filters
were actually honoured rather than trusting the status code.

## Backtested signal value (14,701 matches, scripts/backtest.py)

Ordering in the dossier is EVIDENCE-BASED. Do not reorder to convention
without re-running the backtest.

On the 1,915 matches where the two win rates were within 5 points:

| signal | accuracy | z | read as |
|---|---|---|---|
| avg_round_diff | 57.4% | 6.2 | real, and near-duplicate of round_win_pct |
| round_win_pct | 57.0% | 5.9 | **the strongest usable signal** |
| h2h | 59.2% | 5.9 | real; DIRECTION only, magnitude is meaningless |
| form_last5 | 53.2% | 2.7 | does NOT survive six-signal correction |
| win_rate | 50.3% | 0.3 | null by construction — the internal check |
| random_control | 48.7% | −1.3 | null — confirms the harness |

H2H's accuracy is FLAT across gap quintiles (61.6/57.9/62.4/63.0/60.4). That
normally means noise, but here the gap magnitude reflects how many prior
meetings exist rather than effect strength, so the usual heuristic does not
apply. Read direction, not margin.

**This predicts outcomes, not prices.** A signal that beats a coin flip can
still lose money if the market already prices it. Beating the closing price
needs more settled Kalshi history than exists yet.

## Kalshi status vocabulary (probed 2026-09)

The QUERY token and the RETURNED value differ, which is confusing enough to
write down:

- Query with `status=open` / `closed` / `settled`. `finalized`,
  `determined` and `inactive` are 400 errors as query values.
- Rows come back with `status` of `active`, `inactive` or `finalized`.
  `v_slate` filters on `active`; settled rows are stored as `finalized`.
- `result` is `yes` / `no`, populated only once settled.

**`kalshi_markets.team_id` must be populated or price lookups silently
return nothing.** It existed unwritten for weeks; `decisions.market_prob_for`
and `vs_market.py` both join on it and both reported "no data" rather than
"not linked". `phase1_sync` now fills it every pass, and
`scripts/link_market_teams.py` backfills. **A NULL column in a WHERE clause
returns no rows, and no rows looks exactly like no data** — when a query
comes back empty, check the join before concluding the data is missing.

## The slow thing is always the unlogged thing

Every hang in this project has been a loop with no progress output, and
each was found only by someone interrupting it:

- bo3 pagination refetching page one forever (silent)
- ~3,000 sequential team inserts against Neon (silent)
- the settled sweep: two row-by-row executes per market, ~24,000 round
  trips for 12k markets, about twelve minutes of nothing (silent)

**Batch every write, and print progress on any loop that can exceed a few
seconds.** Either alone is insufficient — a fast silent job is fine until
the data grows, and a slow logged job is at least diagnosable. Chunk-commit
so an interrupt keeps the work already done.

## The benchmark price is the last quote BEFORE THE MATCH STARTED

Not the latest snapshot, and not the last one before `close_time`. Both
were tried and both flatter the market:

- **Latest snapshot.** After settlement the book empties to `yes_bid=0`,
  `yes_ask=100` on both sides. Its mid is 50c, normalising to exactly 0.5
  and an overround of exactly 100 — a market with no opinion, on every
  event.
- **Last quote before `close_time`.** These markets close EARLY, when a
  winner is declared, so that quote can come from mid-match or after the
  result is obvious: 99/100 against 0/1, which also sums to exactly 100.
  Comparing a forecast against a price that already knows the outcome makes
  every signal look worthless — it produced 0% on disagreements.

Use `captured_at < scheduled_at`. That is the standard closing line: what
the market thought before any play. Report the quote's lead time too — a
near-zero median means the "line" is a last-second price, not a line.

**Overround means the ASK-SUM.** `v_slate` defines it as `SUM(yes_ask)`,
and the 104-186 baseline was measured that way. The sum of two MIDS is ~100
for any two-sided book by construction and says nothing about quality —
warning on "mid-sum == 100" fires on every healthy market. Do not mix them.

A genuinely broken book shows up as an empty quote (0/100), a post-result
quote (99/100 vs 0/1), or a nominal one (1/99); reject those on the
book itself, not on a derived overround.

A quote is only a price if the book is two-sided AND the spread is narrow
enough to carry information — `MAX_BENCHMARK_SPREAD = 25c` in
`scripts/vs_market.py`. Wider than that, the mid is an artefact of where
the bounds happen to sit.

**Instrument the instrument.** The lead-time diagnostic added to catch this
was itself broken — `captured_at` was never SELECTed, so it reported 0
minutes on every row. A diagnostic that cannot distinguish "zero" from
"missing" is not a diagnostic; make absence render as absence.

## When a query returns nothing, check the join first

Three separate features reported "no data" this month when the data was
there. Each read as a fact about the world:

- `kalshi_markets.team_id` was never written, so every price lookup joining
  on it came back empty — decisions recorded a NULL market price for weeks.
- `v_unresolved` filters `status IN ('active','open')`, so ~6,000 settled
  events were never offered to the resolver.
- Settled `scheduled_at` was ~48h wrong, so the fixture window searched the
  wrong day.

None errored. **An empty result is a claim about your query before it is a
claim about the world.**

## Source trust order

`bo3.gg` → `HLTV` → `scores24` → `tips.gg` → `egamersworld`

This mirrors Kalshi's own declared settlement precedence. On disagreement,
store the highest-trust value AND write a `source_conflicts` row. Never
silently pick a winner.

## Degradation

Missing data renders as an explicit gap, never a silent omission and never a
guess. A partial dossier is correct behaviour. Player identity is resolved
only within a team-context candidate pool; an ambiguous match shows both
candidates and binds neither.

## Current phase

**Phase 3 (stats)** — Tier-1 statistics over the backfilled bo3 data.
Phases 0, 1 and 1.5 have shipped: the collector runs on a schedule, 16k
matches with per-map results are loaded, and aliases are harvested.

In scope right now:

```
edgedesk/stats/core.py   Stat, n_eff, shrink, decay  (done)
edgedesk/stats/team.py   win/forfeit/form/fatigue    (done)
edgedesk/stats/maps.py   round pct, diff, map pool   (done)
edgedesk/stats/h2h.py    head-to-head                (done)
edgedesk/queries.py      row-fetching for the above  (done)
scripts/dossier.py       render a full dossier       (done)
edgedesk/stats/scoring.py  brier, calibration, fees  (done)
scripts/decide.py        log a decision              (done)
scripts/review.py        score + calibration report  (done)
edgedesk/web/            Flask app, server-rendered  (done)
scripts/serve.py         run it locally              (done)
```

**Charts are inline SVG, not a library.** Geometry lives in
`edgedesk/web/charts.py` as pure tested functions; templates only turn the
numbers into elements. No ECharts, no CDN — layout maths that is computed
can be tested, and a bar longer than its box is a chart that lies.

Colours are diverging (blue `#3987e5` positive / red `#e66767` negative,
gray zero) because the reader's job is polarity. The pair was validated
against the app surface `#171a21` and passes all six checks (CVD ΔE 19.2,
normal-vision 29.0, contrast ≥3:1). Every mark is direct-labelled with its
value and n, so colour never carries meaning alone.

**The web layer computes nothing.** Routes read through `edgedesk.queries`
and compute through `edgedesk.stats`. A number in a template that is not a
`Stat` bypassed the display contract and is a bug. Templates render figures
only via the `_macros.html` `stat()` macro, and `test_web.py` inspects the
rendered HTML per table cell to enforce it.

**Log the no-bets.** A log of only placed bets is a biased sample of the
decisions made and will flatter the tool. Every decision stores the market's
implied probability at logging time — unrecoverable afterwards, and the only
benchmark that makes an absolute Brier score mean anything.

**Forfeits are never scored.** Kalshi resolves a pre-game forfeit to Fair
Market Price, so the position unwinds at the prevailing price rather than
winning or losing. Those rows are recorded as `fmp` and excluded.

Do **not** add HLTV scrapers or Flask routes yet. Anything needing player or
roster data is Phase 2 and returns `Stat.unavailable` for now.

`queries.py` does SQL and computes nothing; `stats/` computes and does no
SQL. Keeping that split sharp is what lets every statistic be tested without
a database.

**Every statistic returns a `Stat`**, never a bare float. A `Stat` cannot be
built without its sample size, which is how the display contract (rule 4) is
enforced structurally instead of by remembering. Missing data is
`Stat.unavailable(reason)` — never 0.0, never omitted.

Phase 1.5 is now largely done: map ingest, alias harvesting, monitoring and
the historical backfill are all in place.

**Condition fallbacks on the outcome, not on scarcity.** The wide-window
retry was gated on the narrow window being empty; after backfilling 16k
matches it never was, the fallback became dead code, and resolved pairs
regressed to queued. Nothing errored.

**Scripts must be exercised, not just their imports.** `phase1_sync.py`
shipped for weeks with `main()` calling a `sync()` that was never written —
every run without `--resolve-only` raised NameError, and the whole test suite
passed because it only covered the pure modules the script imports. Any
script with real logic gets a test that runs it against fakes.

## Working with two developers

- The repo has two contributors. Before creating a file, check whether it
  already exists on another branch or in an open PR.
- **Never edit a migration that has been merged.** Write a new numbered file.
- Prefer small, reviewable diffs. If a change touches more than about six
  files, that is a signal to split it, not to explain it in the PR body.
- When you change a threshold, a weight, or anything deciding *which* rows get
  used, state what you validated against and the before/after numbers.
- Conventions for branches, commits, PRs and database branching are in
  `CONTRIBUTING.md`. Setup and troubleshooting are in `docs/ONBOARDING.md`.
  The system's mental model is in `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`.

## High-risk files

Changes here get reviewed line by line, and an agent should say what it is
about to change before changing it:

- `edgedesk/resolve/fixtures.py` — a wrong link produces a confident dossier
  about the wrong match. Failing to bind is a nuisance; binding wrongly is the
  tool lying. Raise the scorer, never lower `ACCEPT`.
- `migrations/*.sql` — forward-only, applied once, already run against real
  databases.
- Anything writing to a `*_snapshots` table — must be an INSERT.

## Testing

`python -m pytest tests/ -q` — currently **60 passing**. Parser tests run
against saved fixtures in
`tests/fixtures/`. When you add a source, capture a real response as a fixture
first, then write the parser against it. A parser that returns `None` instead
of raising on a shape change is the failure mode these tests exist to catch.

## Conventions

- Prices stored as **integer cents**, never floats. `to_cents()` normalises
  the API's mixed dollar/cent representation.
- All timestamps `timestamptz`, stored UTC. Display timezone is config.
- Every collector run writes a `collection_runs` row, success or failure.
- Commit per logical unit of work, not per file.

## Keeping the three rule files in sync

`CLAUDE.md`, `.cursorrules` and `.cursor/rules/edgedesk.mdc` carry the same
content for Claude Code and Cursor respectively. `CLAUDE.md` is the source of
truth — edit it, then regenerate the other two:

```bash
tail -n +2 CLAUDE.md > .cursorrules
{ printf -- '---\ndescription: Edge Desk project rules\nalwaysApply: true\n---\n\n'; \
  tail -n +2 CLAUDE.md; } > .cursor/rules/edgedesk.mdc
```

They had already drifted once. If you edit one directly, the other developer's
agent is working from different rules than yours.
